WEBVTT

intro-1
00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:08.165
The Effectiveness page answers
one question for administrators:

intro-2
00:00:08.165 --> 00:00:10.459
are the CDS alerts actually helping?

intro-3
00:00:10.459 --> 00:00:14.665
It pairs published benchmark results
with live clinician feedback,

intro-4
00:00:14.665 --> 00:00:16.959
and it shows aggregate figures only.

intro-5
00:00:16.959 --> 00:00:20.082
No patient, case, or free
text ever appears here.

benchmark-1
00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:27.195
Benchmark results come from
published evaluation cards:

benchmark-2
00:00:27.195 --> 00:00:31.269
care-gap precision with and without
the pre-publish gate and recall,

benchmark-3
00:00:31.269 --> 00:00:34.504
each with its confidence
interval, plus cost per case.

partition-1
00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:45.849
Live feedback is filtered here
to one synthetic demo facility.

partition-2
00:00:45.849 --> 00:00:51.132
Every alert lands in exactly one cell:
accepted, dismissed, reviewed, pending,

partition-3
00:00:51.132 --> 00:00:53.028
expired, or resolved in EHR,

partition-4
00:00:53.028 --> 00:00:57.363
which counts alerts the EHR closed
before any clinician decided.

rate-1
00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:07.102
When a group is large enough, you get the
acceptance rate with a 95% confidence

rate-2
00:01:07.102 --> 00:01:10.217
interval, plus the median time to review.

rate-3
00:01:10.217 --> 00:01:14.396
Here, 46 of 67 decided alerts
were accepted, about 69%,

rate-4
00:01:14.396 --> 00:01:17.359
with a median review time of 1.3 hours.

suppression-1
00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:21.168
Small cells are suppressed.

suppression-2
00:01:21.168 --> 00:01:23.698
Any count from 1 to 10
shows as less than 11,

suppression-3
00:01:23.698 --> 00:01:26.735
a total that would give a
hidden count away is masked,

suppression-4
00:01:26.735 --> 00:01:30.333
and the rate is withheld so the
small count can't be worked out.

transport-1
00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:40.284
Runtime settings are admin only.

transport-2
00:01:40.284 --> 00:01:43.261
Each agent's LLM transport
is realtime or batch.

transport-3
00:01:43.261 --> 00:01:47.354
Batch costs about half as much and
applies to scheduled runs only,

transport-4
00:01:47.354 --> 00:01:50.765
and every agent shows the
transport actually in effect.

flag-1
00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:57.036
On this local demo stack the deployment's
batch flag is on, so batch can be chosen.

flag-2
00:01:57.036 --> 00:01:58.384
Where the flag is off,

flag-3
00:01:58.384 --> 00:02:01.999
the option is disabled and the
server refuses a batch save.

save-1
00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:13.756
Saving stores the choice and
pushes it to the agents service.

save-2
00:02:13.756 --> 00:02:18.131
The safety gate still decides: live
PHI stays on realtime until the batch

save-3
00:02:18.131 --> 00:02:22.446
retention safeguards are in place and
the routing policy approves batch,

save-4
00:02:22.446 --> 00:02:24.244
and the page says exactly why.

closing-1
00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:29.979
Saving changes configuration only.

closing-2
00:02:29.979 --> 00:02:31.769
It doesn't start an agent run,

closing-3
00:02:31.769 --> 00:02:35.290
and the stored setting records
which admin made the change.

disclosure-1
00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:40.226
A note on this recording: the
narration uses a synthetic voice.

disclosure-2
00:02:40.226 --> 00:02:43.702
The benchmark results are
aggregate figures from a public,

disclosure-3
00:02:43.702 --> 00:02:48.497
de-identified research dataset, and all
other data shown is synthetic demo data,

disclosure-4
00:02:48.497 --> 00:02:50.355
not a live customer deployment.
